  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 320 AM EDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nSatellite imagery early this morning depicts an expansive area of \nstratus/fog across northwest Ohio, with widespread dense fog \ndeveloping across south central Lower Michigan and northwest Ohio. \nWeak easterly flow associated with eastern portions of low level \nanticyclone will support westward migration of this stratus/fog into \nfar northeast Indiana over the next several hours. A Dense Fog \nAdvisory has issued earlier this morning for Hillsdale Co MI and all \nof NW Ohio counties, and based on current trends, a westward \nexpansion will likely be required. With expansive low level \nanticyclone in place and very weak low level flow, diurnal \nmixing will likely be slow to erode stratus/fog, with areas of \nfog persisting through the 14 to 15Z timeframe.\n\nOtherwise for today will be monitoring the progress of a progressive \nmid/upper level short wave trough across the Upper Midwest this\nmorning as it reaches the western Great Lakes region. A narrow \naxis of mid level moisture/patchy mid level clouds will likely \nremain north of the local area while another area of mid clouds \nassociated with mid level short wave progressing through mean \nupper ridging will largely remain displaced to the west from the\nCentral Plains to the Mid MS Valley. A persistence forecast \nlargely utilized for high temperature forecast today as little \nthermal advections have been noted over past 24 hours with \nsimilar poor mixing profiles today and similar good amounts of\ninsolation.\n\nPassage of the Great Lakes short wave this evening will allow for \nreinforcement and southwestward shift to low level anticyclone \nacross the region. This should yield a weak northeasterly low level \nflow profile for tonight which once again raises the question of \nsome enhancement to fog development downwind of eastern Great Lakes. \nGiven northeast trajectories, greatest confidence in fog development \ntonight is far NE IN/NW OH mainly along and south of US Route 24, \nbut some patchy fog will be possible once again for many areas east \nof I-69. THe fog may become locally dense once again early Thursday \nmorning.\n\nA stable wave pattern will maintain through the first part of the \nweekend with an eventual shift of longwave upper ridge axis \neast of the local area by 2nd half of the weekend. This should \nallow for Sunday to realize the warmest temperatures of the \nperiod in the low to mid 70s. Longwave predictability begins to \nbreak down early next week with large discrepancies in how \nphasing is depicted with western CONUS closed upper height \nperturbations among various deterministic/ensemble guidance. \nSome indications that stronger frontal forcing will develop \nnorthwest of the local area late weekend/early next week as \nsouthwest CONUS upper wave pushes east and dampens, but given \nuncertainty in progression and depth of this wave as it reaches \ncentral CONUS argues for maintenance of mainly dry forecast at \nthis time with expectation that frontal forcing will wane\nthrough time. Blended guidance idea of trend back to cooler \nconditions early to mid next week still appears reasonable.",
